WEBVTT

00:00:00.015 --> 00:00:02.660
Aesop’s Fables. Part 8.

00:00:02.660 --> 00:00:04.256
The Gnat and the Bull.

00:00:04.256 --> 00:00:10.607
A Gnat alighted on one of the horns of a Bull, and remained sitting there for a considerable time.

00:00:10.607 --> 00:00:17.873
When it had rested sufficiently and was about to fly away, it said to the Bull, "Do you mind if I go now?"

00:00:17.873 --> 00:00:28.028
The Bull merely raised his eyes and remarked, without interest, "It's all one to me; I didn't notice when you came, and I shan't know when you go away."

00:00:28.028 --> 00:00:33.311
We may often be of more consequence in our own eyes than in the eyes of our neighbours.

00:00:33.311 --> 00:00:35.311
The Bear and the Travellers.

00:00:35.311 --> 00:00:40.888
Two Travellers were on the road together, when a Bear suddenly appeared on the scene.

00:00:40.888 --> 00:00:48.291
Before he observed them, one made for a tree at the side of the road, and climbed up into the branches and hid there.

00:00:48.291 --> 00:00:57.140
The other was not so nimble as his companion; and, as he could not escape, he threw himself on the ground and pretended to be dead.

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:06.550
The Bear came up and sniffed all round him, but he kept perfectly still and held his breath: for they say that a bear will not touch a dead body.

00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:09.801
The Bear took him for a corpse, and went away.

00:01:09.801 --> 00:01:19.346
When the coast was clear, the Traveller in the tree came down, and asked the other what it was the Bear had whispered to him when he put his mouth to his ear.

00:01:19.346 --> 00:01:26.420
The other replied, "He told me never again to travel with a friend who deserts you at the first sign of danger."

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:29.722
Misfortune tests the sincerity of friendship.

00:01:29.722 --> 00:01:31.566
The Slave and the Lion.

00:01:31.566 --> 00:01:41.571
A Slave ran away from his master, by whom he had been most cruelly treated, and, in order to avoid capture, betook himself into the desert.

00:01:41.571 --> 00:01:49.343
As he wandered about in search of food and shelter, he came to a cave, which he entered and found to be unoccupied.

00:01:49.343 --> 00:01:58.633
Really, however, it was a Lion's den, and almost immediately, to the horror of the wretched fugitive, the Lion himself appeared.

00:01:58.633 --> 00:02:12.988
The man gave himself up for lost: but, to his utter astonishment, the Lion, instead of springing upon him and devouring him, came and fawned upon him, at the same time whining and lifting up his paw.

00:02:12.988 --> 00:02:20.585
Observing it to be much swollen and inflamed, he examined it and found a large thorn embedded in the ball of the foot.

00:02:20.585 --> 00:02:27.888
He accordingly removed it and dressed the wound as well as he could: and in course of time it healed up completely.

00:02:27.888 --> 00:02:36.028
The Lion's gratitude was unbounded; he looked upon the man as his friend, and they shared the cave for some time together.

00:02:36.028 --> 00:02:45.561
A day came, however, when the Slave began to long for the society of his fellow-men, and he bade farewell to the Lion and returned to the town.

00:02:45.561 --> 00:02:58.755
Here he was presently recognised and carried off in chains to his former master, who resolved to make an example of him, and ordered that he should be thrown to the beasts at the next public spectacle in the theatre.

00:02:58.755 --> 00:03:10.108
On the fatal day the beasts were loosed into the arena, and among the rest a Lion of huge bulk and ferocious aspect; and then the wretched Slave was cast in among them.

00:03:10.108 --> 00:03:20.016
What was the amazement of the spectators, when the Lion after one glance bounded up to him and lay down at his feet with every expression of affection and delight!

00:03:20.016 --> 00:03:22.226
It was his old friend of the cave!

00:03:22.226 --> 00:03:34.627
The audience clamoured that the Slave's life should be spared: and the governor of the town, marvelling at such gratitude and fidelity in a beast, decreed that both should receive their liberty.

00:03:34.627 --> 00:03:36.250
The Flea and the Man.

00:03:36.865 --> 00:03:47.560
A Flea bit a Man, and bit him again, and again, till he could stand it no longer, but made a thorough search for it, and at last succeeded in catching it.

00:03:47.560 --> 00:04:00.630
Holding it between his finger and thumb, he said—or rather shouted, so angry was he—"Who are you, pray, you wretched little creature, that you make so free with my person?"

00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:09.137
The Flea, terrified, whimpered in a weak little voice, "Oh, sir! pray let me go; don't kill me!

00:04:09.137 --> 00:04:12.696
I am such a little thing that I can't do you much harm."

00:04:12.696 --> 00:04:23.231
But the Man laughed and said, "I am going to kill you now, at once: whatever is bad has got to be destroyed, no matter how slight the harm it does."

00:04:23.231 --> 00:04:25.723
Do not waste your pity on a scamp.

00:04:25.723 --> 00:04:27.307
The Bee and Jupiter.

00:04:27.307 --> 00:04:39.577
A Queen Bee from Hymettus flew up to Olympus with some fresh honey from the hive as a present to Jupiter, who was so pleased with the gift that he promised to give her anything she liked to ask for.

00:04:39.577 --> 00:04:46.759
She said she would be very grateful if he would give stings to the bees, to kill people who robbed them of their honey.

00:04:46.759 --> 00:04:56.394
Jupiter was greatly displeased with this request, for he loved mankind: but he had given his word, so he said that stings they should have.

00:04:56.394 --> 00:05:04.846
The stings he gave them, however, were of such a kind that whenever a bee stings a man the sting is left in the wound and the bee dies.

00:05:04.846 --> 00:05:08.758
Evil wishes, like fowls, come home to roost.
